JNI module for the Qore programming language.

http://qore.org

Building
--------

Requirements:
- CMake 3.5 or later
- Qore 2.0 or later
- Java JDK 21 or later
- BZip2 development libraries

To build:

    mkdir build
    cd build
    cmake ..
    make
    sudo make install

macOS
-----

To ensure that JNI programs work on macOS, ensure that your installed JDK declares the
"BundledApp" and "JNI" capabilities in the Info.plist file for the installed JDK.

For example, the latest version for 1.8 is at the time of writing this part of the
README in the following file:

       /Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/jdk1.8.0_162.jdk/Contents/Info.plist

Edit the Info.plist file to look as follows:

                <key>JVMCapabilities</key>
                <array>
                        <string>BundledApp</string>
                        <string>CommandLine</string>
                        <string>JNI</string>
                </array>

After this JNI programs should work normally.

Documentation qualification
---------------------------

Configure with -DQORE_JNI_STRICT_DOCS=ON to reject Doxygen warnings. Install the
Qore development package with its language and module reference indexes, then run:

    cmake --build build --target docs
    python3 -B -W error test/test-doc-index.py --build-dir build -v

The checks cover the Java and Qore APIs, legacy import anchors, dependency links,
JMS table rendering, and rejection of unresolved references. Documentation links
to XML and SOAP APIs use the separately published XML module reference.

JNI reference qualification
---------------------------

Run the reference and exception regressions with checked JNI enabled:

    QORE_JNI_JVM_ARGS=-Xcheck:jni qore -b --enable-debug -l build/jni-api-2.0.qmod test/jni-reference-safety.qtest -v

The suite covers repeated array allocation, failed constructors, nullable exception
fields, argument conversion failures, Qore object arguments, owned callback results, and callbacks whose
owning program has already closed. Run `ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure`
for deterministic cleanup ownership tests, including concurrent claims, registration
at shutdown, and construction that finishes after its cleanup handle was claimed.
The native shutdown path drains wrappers even when Java has not collected them.
CTest also imports the AWT drag-and-drop event class with checked JNI and an explicit
headless JVM. This verifies the static graphics-environment query used while importing
spreadsheet APIs on servers without a display.
The main
jni.qtest suite returns normally so native teardown is included in memory checks;
its abrupt-exit behavior is tested in a separate subprocess. When running Valgrind
with the optional Python module, use PYTHONMALLOC=malloc to expose individual Python
allocations instead of its pooled allocator.
